## PR #50 lessons (do not repeat)

Source: PR #50 review fixes on `feat/platform-admin-hospitals`.

### Module structure — no "portable root" split

- Implementation lives directly in `src/modules/<name>/` (backend and frontend). Do **not** split a module across a separate top-level `src/<name>/` "portable" folder plus a thin `src/modules/<name>/index.ts` that just re-exports it — that's two places to look for one module.
- Reference shape: `mch` — everything (`<name>.constants.ts`, `<name>.types.ts`, `api/`, `hooks/`, pages/routes as applicable) sits directly under `src/modules/<name>/`.
- One constants file, one types file per module — not `constants/index.ts` + `constants/<feature>.ts` split for no reason.

### Constants ↔ types — no circular imports

- If a module's `<name>.constants.ts` and `<name>.types.ts` import from each other, one of them must become a pure leaf (zero imports) and the other imports from it one-directionally.
- Put the raw `as const` value objects (and any type derived via `(typeof X)[keyof typeof X]`) in the same file as their source constant, not split across the two files by convention alone.

### Route registration

- `*.routes.ts` never contains a hardcoded API base path repeated per route (`/api/platform/...` on every line). Define `<MODULE>_API_BASE` in `<module>.constants.ts` and build every path from it.
- `*.routes.ts` never contains an inline handler — not even a trivial one like `/health`. Every route delegates to a named handler in `*.controller.ts`, matching the rest of the module.

### Service file naming for a split-out concern

- When a focused piece of a module's service is pulled into its own file, name it `<concern>.service.ts` — **drop the module-name prefix** (the folder already scopes it). Reference: `identityProfile.service.ts`, `relatedPersons.service.ts`, `sse.service.ts`. Bad: `org.invite.ts`, `org.auth.ts`.

### Constants dedup — check across files, not just within one

- The same hardcoded string/comparison (e.g. a role check) duplicated across **multiple files** is the same bug as duplicating it within one file — grep the literal across the whole repo, not just the file under review, before deciding a constant isn't warranted.

### Integration test DB mocks — default row completeness

- A **default** (non-overridden) mock row for a table must include every field that *any* real call site destructures from it — not just the fields the first caller happened to need. A missing field silently becomes `undefined`/`NaN` in the handler instead of failing loudly, and can pass every existing test while still 500ing on any endpoint nobody happened to exercise with default mock state. Caught by a full-route sweep test (`openapi-get-sweep.integration.test.ts`), not by the endpoint's own test.
- When one query shape isn't enough because a single code path issues several differently-shaped queries against the same table in sequence (e.g. a role-gate check, then a user-count query, then a grouped-count query, all against `user_roles`), use a per-table **queued** override (`createQueuedDrizzleOverride` in `db-mock-impl.ts`) rather than trying to cram every shape into one static row.

### AJV messages — don't break existing message-content assertions

- Before rewriting an `errorMessage` to be more human-friendly, grep for existing tests asserting on the *old* message content (e.g. `toMatch(/uuid|format/)`). A `format: uuid` field's custom message should still signal "UUID" if a test already keys off that word — `"X must be a valid UUID"` satisfies both the human-readability bar and the existing test.

